 \section{Introduction}
 
-The urgency of mitigating global warming necessitates a rapid energy transition to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) \cite{messerli2019global}. Since energy consumption is the primary source of global greenhouse gas emissions \cite{BOGDANOV2021120467}, a transformation of energy systems is essential. This transition is not solely a technological endeavor \cite{CHEN20191564}; it also encompasses critical economic dimensions, among which the weighted average cost of capital (WACC) is a critical metric \cite{STEWART2022111880}. It represents the average return required by capital providers, balancing the costs of debt and equity financing \cite{BACHNER2019100373}. The environmental consequences of this financial indicator are profound; modeling shows that high capital costs in developing economies can reduce green energy production by 35\% and delay the achievement of net-zero emissions by a full decade \cite{ameli2021higher}.
